FFmpeg 9.0.2's HLS demuxer cannot seek in a live playlist that slides (no EXT-X-PLAYLIST-TYPE:EVENT, no ENDLIST: a
YouTube live stream's DVR window): its first_timestamp is that of the first packet read, which is segments from the
playlist's start, so a target maps to the wrong segment, and a seek fails (EPERM). The window is what a live stream's
timeline in Nits is made of (LiveStream), and is seeked in.

Three parts, restricted to libavformat/hls.c:
 - Upstream FFmpeg master's series of 2026-07-08 (not in 9.0.2, nor in release/9.0), from "use av_rescale_q instead of
   doing only denominator" to "refresh stale playlist when seeking" (e822dd253b, 2526ac4771, ede944b362, 84bc576371,
   848fb754dd, f6fa0d3fda, b5402d1ecf, ee498f5e82): seeking within live playlists.
 - Ours, first_timestamp: the series moves first_timestamp as the playlist slides only when the playlist it came from
   (the one of the first packet read) is the one loaded. With a master playlist the variant playing is another one,
   and the first is never loaded again: first_timestamp stood still, and seeks mapped to the wrong segment. It now
   tells the media sequence number of the segment it is the start of (first_timestamp_seq), and any needed playlist
   advances it by the durations of the segments its window dropped; a slide longer than the time that has passed (and the
   playlist lists) or a step back of more than the playlist lists is another numbering (variants need not share a base),
   and it follows the time; a small step back (a CDN serving an older playlist) changes nothing.
 - Ours, the window: after every load of the playlist of the variant playing (not of a rendition of it: an audio
   rendition has a window of its own, which must not end the stream's), a live one (not finished, not EVENT) publishes
   its window as the format context's start_time (the first segment's start; it moves as the playlist slides) and
   duration (the length of its segments), and tags the context with "hls_live" = the media sequence number of its first
   segment. When the playlist ends, the last window stays and the tag is removed. An EVENT playlist's duration was
   published already. The player reads them on the demuxer's thread, right after av_read_frame.

Applied by scripts/build_dependencies.sh to FFmpeg 9.0.2; drop the first part once the bundled FFmpeg has it.

